WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.550
Recently when I was at Harvard Business School
with 144 other entrepreneurs who ran very

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:12.940
successful businesses, valued from $100 million
upwards of $4 billion, there was one thing

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:13.940
everybody had in common.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:18.330
When we were off and talking and were just
chatting with each other, everyone had a horror

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:19.410
story.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.300
Everybody had a story of how close they were
of going out of business.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:27.710
When I tell you everybody, I mean everybody
had a horror story or at one point they did,

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:30.290
and they had to make a comeback.

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:31.290
Every single person.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:36.750
I will tell you here for you watching, if
you have any aspirations for running a very

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:40.730
successful business one day or being very
successful as an entrepreneur yourself, you

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:46.590
need to know that it is guaranteed you will
face some scary moments in business.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:51.690
And you can overcome them, and that is when
we really find out what you're really made

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:52.690
up of.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:54.670
That's when everybody finds out what's you're
made up of.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:56.100
Just so you know that.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.680
Anybody that you meet that's successful is
able to face those horror stories and overcome

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:00.680
them.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:05.080
So, I'm going to share with you a few of my
horror stories and hopefully this will shed

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:09.719
some light to you to realize you're not alone
by yourself and other people go through this

00:01:09.719 --> 00:01:10.719
as well.

00:01:10.719 --> 00:01:11.999
Most of them are going to be a few experiences
I've had.

00:01:11.999 --> 00:01:14.340
I think there's like 11 or 12 of them that
I personally experienced.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.900
One of them will be this.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:24.610
One of the scariest things, one of the scariest
things in business is when you declare your

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:33.020
intentions to the world that you're starting
a business and you have opposition 100%.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:35.240
No one believes you're going to be successful
in business.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:36.240
Why?

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.729
Because you've never ran a business before.

00:01:37.729 --> 00:01:40.619
Why would they believe you're going to be
successful in business.

00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:44.729
And what I call this experience is, The I
Told You So Nightmare.

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:48.210
Let me tell you what's the I Told You So Nightmare.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:54.569
I cannot tell you how many nights I went to
sleep, sleepless nights, I would have nightmares

00:01:54.569 --> 00:01:59.259
about those people that told me not to do
it, and I'm facing them and they're telling

00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:01.869
me, "I told you so!"

00:02:01.869 --> 00:02:04.029
I told you you should have never done this.

00:02:04.029 --> 00:02:06.139
I told you this wasn't going to work out.

00:02:06.139 --> 00:02:07.619
I told you so.

00:02:07.619 --> 00:02:10.729
Oh, those nightmares were unbelievably painful.

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:12.590
Let me tell you.

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:17.310
And these I told you so faces, some of them
were relatives, some of them were friends,

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:20.290
some of them were enemies, who said, "I told
you so."

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:24.269
And I'd wake up in the middle of the night
- I can't tell you how many times - my entire

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:25.750
shirt was wet.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:30.870
I'm in bed, and had to go to the bathroom
and splash some cold water on my face and

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:32.299
I'm like, "It's just a nightmare.

00:02:32.299 --> 00:02:33.299
It's just a nightmare.

00:02:33.299 --> 00:02:34.299
It's okay.

00:02:34.299 --> 00:02:35.299
It's okay.

00:02:35.299 --> 00:02:36.299
Go back to sleep."

00:02:36.299 --> 00:02:37.299
Tried going back to sleep, and I couldn't
do that.

00:02:37.299 --> 00:02:39.120
I had to get back to work, because I'm thinking
I'm about to go out of business, because someone's

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:40.340
going to tell me "I told you so."

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.569
It's a very scary moment in business.

00:02:42.569 --> 00:02:44.249
You will experience many of that.

00:02:44.249 --> 00:02:45.409
It's gonna happen.

00:02:45.409 --> 00:02:47.799
But it's just a part of imagination.

00:02:47.799 --> 00:02:49.260
It's not a reality.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.409
But it's going to mess with you.

00:02:50.409 --> 00:02:51.409
Another one is this.

00:02:51.409 --> 00:02:52.599
Here's another one.

00:02:52.599 --> 00:02:57.200
Another one is, when you actually start partnering
up with a company.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:00.000
I remember one time they hired me, a startup
hired me.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.950
They did technology products, which was like
the next up and coming Yahoo! and they were

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:05.950
based out of Santa Ana.

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:11.459
They hired me to train their salespeople and
I came through and I brought on board 100

00:03:11.459 --> 00:03:14.670
sales people that was training in the Southern
California marketplace.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:17.590
And I'll never forget on a Monday morning,
I'm going there to collect all the checks

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:22.090
to come and do a sales rally and give these
checks away to the salespeople, and Monday

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:25.299
morning I'm going up the elevator with the
president, a guy named Ray.

00:03:25.299 --> 00:03:27.379
At that time I'm going up stairs.

00:03:27.379 --> 00:03:30.689
We knock on the door, he puts the key in to
open the headquarters.

00:03:30.689 --> 00:03:31.700
He can't open it.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:34.420
Janitor comes and said they pulled everything
out over the weekend.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:36.319
He was like, what are you talking about?

00:03:36.319 --> 00:03:38.650
We opened it, it was as if everything was
slow motion.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:42.200
The door opened like a movie dah dah dah dah
dah.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.730
Everything was pulled out, except the logo.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:46.540
I said, wait a minute.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:49.299
Why's the logo - everything was pulled out.

00:03:49.299 --> 00:03:51.520
Every check that was uncashed.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:55.019
Every check that was sitting on the table
to deliver to our salesforce.

00:03:55.019 --> 00:03:56.970
We went to the nearest bank.

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:57.970
Nothing could be cashed.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:03:59.109
They closed down the checking account.

00:03:59.109 --> 00:04:00.170
It was done.

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:01.459
We couldn't get ahold of the investor.

00:04:01.459 --> 00:04:02.459
We couldn't get ahold of the founder.

00:04:02.459 --> 00:04:05.459
We couldn't get ahold of nobody with that
company.

00:04:05.459 --> 00:04:06.489
Six months, went out of business.

00:04:06.489 --> 00:04:11.959
That night, I had to go to Conrad's in La
Crescenta Valley and I had to deliver the

00:04:11.959 --> 00:04:14.590
fact that that company went out of business
in front of 100 people.

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:15.689
Let me tell you.

00:04:15.689 --> 00:04:18.920
That drive back from Santa Ana to La Crescenta
Valley with traffic, was about a two and a

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:22.470
half to three hour drive was a very difficult
Monday night drive.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:27.381
And I met with them, some of you may even
watch this, may share it with somebody who

00:04:27.381 --> 00:04:28.670
was part of that meeting.

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:30.240
That was not an easy meeting.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:35.160
But that night, if there's anything I learned
that night is, I learned how to toughen up

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:37.230
and learned to deliver bad news.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:38.230
And I had to make myself.

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:43.480
You think this is bad news, what's bad news
compared to this is if you're in the military

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:47.230
and if you have to go tell somebody's wife
with two kids saying their husband just passed

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:48.950
away, that's bad news.

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:50.130
Your's isn't that bad of news.

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:54.670
I had to go and deliver that news and had
a difficult time sleeping that night.

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:55.670
I felt responsibility.

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:57.980
I felt it was my fault, etc. etc.

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:02.110
But I grew from that moment, delivering bad
news to a company that went out of business.

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:03.790
Another scary moment is when you run out of
money.

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:06.850
I want you to think about this here.

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:10.310
Imagine you start a company with every single
penny you have.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:15.510
Everything from your insurance policies, from
mutual funds, stocks, cash, savings, money

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:19.780
fund accounts - any money market accounts.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:20.780
Anything you can think about.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:24.070
Let's just say you have a million dollars,
and you start this company.

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:26.790
Six months later you're down to half a million
dollars.

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:28.220
Nine months later you're down to $100,000.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:31.820
Twelve months later you're down to $13,000.

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:34.250
You literally have nothing left.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:37.720
Every credit card's been maxed out, and you
only have $13,000 left.

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:43.020
And by the end of the week, all your employees,
your sales reps, your vendors, everybody expects

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:44.720
a payment from you.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:49.240
And if you don't pay them, they're going to
leave you and if you do pay them, you're out

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:50.650
of business.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:53.080
You don't have money to pay your rent, food,
anything.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:54.260
What do you do?

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:55.690
Do you panic?

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:59.030
What do you do at that moment?

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:00.031
See, I experienced that.

00:06:00.031 --> 00:06:03.590
I experienced that in October of 2010.

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:05.330
Let me tell you, it was very scary.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:07.680
Very, very scary.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.610
And you realize what things you need to do
at the moment.

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:12.060
Like the last thing you need to do is panic.

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:14.240
You need to figure out the next steps on what
to do.

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:16.880
But that is a very scary moment in business
that could happen.

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:20.500
And you have to still figure out a way to
get out of a situation like that, which we

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:23.110
ended up getting out of it, and good things
started happening.

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:27.550
Another scary moment is when you start a company.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:29.220
It's a startup.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:31.740
And you don't speak the language of the company.

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:33.210
You don't speak the language of a startup.

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:38.620
So I remember the first time we started our
insurance company, PHP.

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:40.020
And I had no carriers.

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:42.320
I had no insurance company to write their
paperwork up.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:43.320
None.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:44.560
None of them have said yes to me.

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:47.280
Every one of them I met with said no to me.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.220
They said, "How long have you been around?"

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:50.220
I said, "One week."

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:51.220
They said, NO!

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:57.200
So one guy I got on a call with, he finally
said, Yeah, who are you with?

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:58.370
Let's meet in the next two months.

00:06:58.370 --> 00:06:59.370
I said, "No, no, no.

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:01.090
I'd like to meet with you today or tomorrow."

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:02.650
I'm in L.A. He's in Chicago.

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:04.340
He said, "Oh, you're in town?"

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:05.470
I said, "Oh yeah."

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:06.620
He said, "Okay.

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:09.980
If you're around tomorrow, why don't we do
lunch tomorrow at 12:00?"

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:11.740
I said, "No problem."

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:16.940
I get a flight that night, a $1200 flight
to go to Chicago from L.A. I go to Chicago,

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:18.960
12:00, I go to meet him.

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:20.750
This man, his name was Garth.

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:21.750
I shake his hand.

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:22.750
We go upstairs.

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:23.750
One hour we speak.

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:24.750
One hour later, I'm leaving.

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:26.590
I have no idea what he's talking about, the
words he's asking me.

00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:30.030
I don't know a lot of the language that he's
asking me these questions of.

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:31.970
I'm just having a very sincere meeting with
him.

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:34.830
Here's what we're going to do, here's what's
happening with our business.

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:35.830
He shakes my hand.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:37.930
This was a multi-billion dollar company.

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:39.360
He said, "We'll give you the contract.

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:40.460
Let's get the paperwork started."

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:41.460
Right there.

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:42.810
I'm, like, wait a minute, this is really happening.

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:43.810
Yes it is.

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:50.280
I left trying to control my emotions on how
excited I was, as if everything was expected,

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:53.050
and I go down and said, "Oh my gosh.

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:58.620
I cannot believe I was able to get this appointment
and close it, without knowing half the words

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:00.840
he asked me on what it is to run a startup.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:02.130
Do you have this?

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:03.130
Do you have this?

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:04.130
Do you have that?

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:05.710
Who is your general counsel, legal counsel,
accountant?

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:06.710
Are you accrued?

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:08.360
Are you this, are you that?

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:09.360
What's your.

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:10.360
. . everything, I had no idea.

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:13.381
So sometimes a scary thing is you go to a
meeting and the meeting is bigger than you.

00:08:13.381 --> 00:08:14.530
How do you handle a meeting like that.

00:08:14.530 --> 00:08:16.720
You're going to experience some of those as
well.

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:17.720
Next one.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:22.060
The next one was final straw negotiation.

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:24.210
Let me explain to you what I mean by this.

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:28.440
This is when you're negotiating with somebody
and you do not have the leverage over the

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:29.640
other company.

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:35.450
So it is August of 2011.

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:37.950
We're about to go out of business, PHP.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:39.200
We're about to go out of business.

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:40.530
August of 2011.

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:41.530
Nobody knows this.

00:08:41.530 --> 00:08:43.240
I have no carriers.

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:44.430
No carriers.

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:49.360
One of our competitors was going around, you
know, defamation of character, whatever you

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:50.360
want to call it.

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:53.970
They were trying to do whatever they can for
every single carrier to stop representing

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:55.120
us.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:56.900
Everyone dropped.

00:08:56.900 --> 00:08:59.090
Everyone said, "Okay, we're not doing business
with them."

00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:00.090
Except for one company.

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:01.090
And it was AIG.

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:02.780
It was a gentleman named Greg.

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:03.980
I met together with Greg.

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:06.400
We had already known each other and we started
talking.

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:08.440
I had no leverage.

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:10.480
We had nothing going on at that time.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:13.940
And He and I sat down and we had a long conversation
together and then the next level and the next

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:18.120
level and I flew in and we had meetings, long
meetings, long meetings with AIG.

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:20.960
And then by the time we were done, they said
yes.

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:23.220
I had zero leverage in that negotiation.

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:24.700
It was the final straw.

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:28.770
If they say no, next week we're out of business.

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:30.130
Next week we're out of business.

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:31.280
They said yes.

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:32.280
I went back.

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:37.420
I announced it, for the first 90 days it was
absolutely terrible.

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:38.820
Nothing went the right way.

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:43.190
And eventually, we ended up writing more index
universal life with AIG three, four years

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:48.150
in a row, than any other company in the world,
and it turned into a very, very successful

00:09:48.150 --> 00:09:49.150
relationship.

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:53.630
But I had no negotiation leverage when I was
negotiating with these guys at that time.

00:09:53.630 --> 00:09:55.640
Next one is, when a big client changes their
mind.

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:56.640
This happens in sales a lot.

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:01.060
For me it happened early on in my sales career,
when I quit my job and I went in, I remember

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:05.800
I had a sales that I made, the sales call
I made, the commission was $11,000.

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:08.430
And $11,000 at that time was a lot of money.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:14.120
$11,000 was a lot of money to me, because
that $11,000, I was already counting my rent,

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:17.350
my car payment, my debt, my phone, every single
thing.

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:24.170
It was back in the day with Nextel, the way
Nextel worked I was $890, I had to pay that

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:25.170
off.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:26.170
Everything, I was behind.

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:28.220
So I'm counting on this $11,000 check.

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:30.950
I leave the house, I'm so excited, I'm relieved.

00:10:30.950 --> 00:10:32.360
I got breathing room again.

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:39.930
I go in, excited that next day, and then all
of a sudden, I got a call from the client.

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:40.930
And you know you don't want to pick up that
call.

00:10:40.930 --> 00:10:41.930
Because you know exactly who it is.

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:42.930
I pick up the client's call.

00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:44.420
They want to change their mind.

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:45.420
What happened?

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:46.420
We just want to change our mind.

00:10:46.420 --> 00:10:47.610
What happened?

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:52.610
My cousin who does the same thing you do,
he's with the same company you're with.

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:54.420
We didn't know he was with the same company.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:57.210
We called him for advice because we know he
was in the insurance business, and we've chosen

00:10:57.210 --> 00:10:58.660
to go with him.

00:10:58.660 --> 00:10:59.740
But, but.

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:02.350
. . we totally understand, we know what you're
going to say, and his cousin prepped him on

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:05.810
everything I was going to say, so I had no
comeback.

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:07.360
